[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "29.1"]
[White "22.7) The Lopez Formation"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "4k3/pp3ppp/3p4/2p1p3/3PP3/2P5/PP3PPP/4K3 w - - 0 1"]

 { Similarly to the King’s Indian Complex which we studied in Chapter 18, the Lopez Formation is a very flexible structure. The name ‘Lopez Formation’ was introduced by Soltis in the 1970’s and refers to the fact that this structure almost always arises from the Ruy Lopez. In essence, this structure is one in transition. Depending on how White and Black proceed in the centre, this structure will be transformed in one of the following ways: 1. White advances d4-d5 and later c3-c4, obtaining a King’s Indian Type II structure (Chapter 15). 2. White advances d4-d5 and Black replies ...c5-c4, to obtain a Closed Ruy Lopez, which has just been covered. 3. White captures d4xe5 (or d4xc5) and Black recaptures ...d6xe5 (or ...d6xc5), obtaining a typical transformation of the King’s Indian Complex (Chapter 18). 4. Black captures ...e5xd4 and after c3xd4 White proceeds with d4-d5, obtaining an Asymmetric Benoni (Chapter 12). 5. Black captures ...c5xd4 and after c3xd4 White proceeds with d4-d5, obtaining a King’s Indian Type I structure (Chapter 14). 6. Black trades two pawns on d4 with ...c5xd4 followed by ...e5xd4 (or in the other order). The resulting position has not been discussed in previous chapters, and here we will see an example of it. }  
 *

[Event "Chess Structures – A Grandmaster Guide"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/1551633/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "29.2"]
[White "22.7) The Lopez Formation"]
[Black "David Navara vs. Ivan Sokolov"]
[Result "0-1"]

 { David Navara – Ivan Sokolov, Reykjavik 2012 Learning objectives: 1. This game is an example of Black’s central counterplay after a double pawn trade on d4. 2. Note how the e4-pawn becomes a serious target before White can organize an attack against the technically weak d6-pawn. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. O-O Nf6 6. Re1 Bd7 7. c3 g6 8. d4 b5 9. Bb3 Bg7 { So far we are following a standard variation of the Deferred Steinitz Variation, and now: } 10. Bg5 $6 { This is the beginning of a bad plan. } ( { Instead } 10. h3 O-O 11. Nbd2 h6 12. Nf1 Re8 13. Ng3 { is a healthier approach, since the bishop is more useful on c1 than on h4 . } ) 
 ( { PH: Personally I think } 10. Be3 { is best here, only after } Ng4 { going } 11. Bg5 { , while } h6 { is answered by } 11. h3 O-O 12. Nbd2 $14 Re8 13. Bc2 { with a harmonious position. } ) 
O-O 11. Nbd2 Re8 12. h3 h6 13. Bh4 { White’s bishop will be quite useless on this square. } ( { Better was } 13. Be3 $1 { since } exd4 $6 14. cxd4 Nxe4 $2 { is met by: } 15. Nxe4 Rxe4 16. Qc2 $1 Re7 ( { or } 16. Bh4 Qe8 17. Bxh6 $1 { ± } ) 
17. Qxg6 $1 { ± } ) 
Na5 14. Bc2 c5 $1 { And we have obtained our Lopez Formation. Black has made a good choice since he is well prepared if White closes the centre with d4-d5. In addition, the move 14...c5 allows ...Qc7, moving away from the pin and leaving the bishop on h4 doing nothing. } 15. Nb3 $6 { A strange choice that is too slow for the position. } ( { Instead } 15. d5 Qc7 { gives Black a good position, as his fianchettoed bishop makes for an impenetrable kingside. Meanwhile, White’s dark-squared bishop is somewhat misplaced. A possible continuation was } 16. Bxf6 ( { or } 16. Nf1 Nh5 ) 
Bxf6 17. Nf1 Bg7 18. Ne3 Rf8 { when Black plans ...f7-f5 with good kingside play. } ) 
 ( { PH: After } 15. d5 { , } c4 $1 { is even stronger, when } 16. b4 cxb3 17. axb3 Qc7 { White's c3-pawn is rather weak and he would probably have to play the awkward } 18. Re3 { . } ) 
 ( { If } 15. dxc5 dxc5 16. Nf1 Qc7 17. Ne3 Be6 { Black is not worse, as his queenside has already expanded while the d5-square is protected. } ) 
cxd4 16. Nxa5 ( { An option was } 16. cxd4 exd4 17. Nbxd4 Qb6 { though Black is very comfortable and active. For example: } 18. Bg3 ( { or } 18. a4 Nh5 $1 { threatening ...g5 } ) 
 ( { PH: } 18. b3 { stops ...Nc4, but makes the long diagonal vulnerable after } Nh5 $1 ) 
Nc4 19. Rb1 ( { or } 19. b3 Na3 $15 ) 
Rad8 ( { or } 19. Nxa5 Bc8 $5 { followed by ...Bb7 attacking e4 } ) 
 { And now White is defenceless against the threat of 20...d5 , for example: } 20. a4 d5 21. e5 Ne4 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Qxa5 17. cxd4 exd4 $1 { A good decision, opening the position and taking advantage of White’s poorly-arranged pieces. } 18. Nxd4 ( 18. Qxd4 { is met by } g5 $1 19. Bg3 Nxe4 20. Be5 dxe5 21. Qxe4 Qc7 $15 { when White’s compensation is quite insufficient. } ) 
Qb6 $15 { We have reached a critical position and it is time to make an assessment. In the long term White should have a huge advantage because the d6-pawn is a weak isolated backward pawn – an easy target. However, Black’s pieces are arranged so well that it is not clear White will have a long term! Black’s bishop is extremely powerful along the h8-a1 diagonal, pressuring the knight on d4 and the pawn on b2. Meanwhile, White’s bishop on h4 is rather useless and would have been far better placed on b2 or e3. When Black decides to trade both pawns on d4, he must carefully assess whether he will have enough counterplay to compensate for the weak d6-pawn. In this case, Black has made an excellent choice and in fact, he is already better. } 19. Nf3 Be6 { Clearing d7 for the knight. } 20. Nd4 ( { Or } 20. Qd2 Nd7 21. Bd1 Rac8 22. Bg3 Nc5 23. Rc1 Qb7 24. Bc2 d5 $1 { trading the weak d6-pawn: } 25. exd5 Bxd5 $15 ) 
Bc8 $1 { Now the bishop is heading to b7, and suddenly the e4-pawn is far weaker than the d6-pawn. } 21. Qd2 ( { Correct was: } 21. Ne2 $1 Bb7 ( { not } 21. Qd2 Nxe4 $2 22. Nf4 $1 { and White gains counterplay, for example: } Bb7 23. Nd5 Bxd5 24. Qxd5 Nf6 25. Bxf6 Bxf6 26. Bxg6 $1 { ⩲ } ) 
22. Nc3 { Now after either } Rac8 $15 ( { or } 22. Qd2 d5 $5 ) 
 { (Black preserves a comfortable position.) } ) 
Bb7 22. Bxf6 ( 22. f3 $2 { is met by: } Nxe4 $1 23. Bxe4 Qxd4 $17 ) 
Bxf6 23. Rad1 $2 { The last mistake. } ( { White puts up a better fight after } 23. Nf3 $1 Rac8 $1 ( { but not } 23. Rad1 Bxb2 $6 24. Qxh6 $1 { with good compensation } ) 
24. Bb3 Kg7 $17 { when White’s pawns on e4 and b2 are vulnerable. } ) 
d5 $1 { -+ The ‘weak’ d6-pawn arrives to decide the game. Black is winning a pawn with a decisive advantage. } 24. Bb3 ( { The point is that } 24. exd5 $2 { loses a piece after } Rxe1 25. Rxe1 Bxd4 { . } ) 
 ( 24. e5 { is also inadequate: } Bxe5 25. Nf3 Bg7 26. Bb3 d4 $1 { -+ } ) 
Rxe4 25. Rxe4 dxe4 26. Nf5 $5 { A good try in a lost position. } Rd8 $1 ( { But not } 26... gxf5 $2 27. Qxh6 $1 { when Black has nothing better than a draw after: } Bd4 28. Qg5 Kh8 ( 28... Kf8 $6 29. Qxf5 $14 ) 
29. Qh5 Kg8 30. Qg5 $11 ) 
27. Nxh6+ ( { Instantly hopeless is } 27. Qxh6 $2 Rxd1 28. Bxd1 gxf5 { . } ) 
Kg7 28. Qc1 Rxd1+ 29. Bxd1 e3 $1 { Winning a decisive tempo by attacking the knight. } 30. Ng4 exf2+ 31. Kf1 Bd4 { The rest is simple. } 32. Qh6+ Kg8 33. Qf4 Qc6 34. Bf3 Qc4+ 35. Be2 Bxg2+ 36. Kxg2 Qxe2 37. Nh6+ Kh7 { Final remarks 1. The d6-pawn was never a weakness. Instead, the e4-pawn was quite vulnerable, since playing f2-f3 would severely weaken the g1-a7 diagonal. 2. One of White’s biggest problems was not having the dark-squared bishop on b2 or e3, to parry the threats. 3. Black’s counterplay was particularly successful because of White’s poor coordination. The double pawn trade on d4 would not work as well if White could manage to stabilize the position. } 
 0-1

